5. BUSINESS OBJECTIVES
5.1 Business Success Criteria
•	The model should produce useful predictions with acceptable errors.
•	Model performance should be measured using R², MAE and RMSE.
•	The prediction results should be clear and easy to understand.
•	The data preparation and model results should be reproducible.
•	The final solution should provide a simple way to estimate a transaction value.
5.2 Requirements
Data
•	Historical retail sales data (Dataset).
•	Customer information such as quantity, unit_price, discount_pct, date, sales_channel, payment_method and region
•	Sales_amount as the target variable.

5.3 Technical requirements
•	Python and machine learning tools.
•	A trained prediction model.
•	Tables or charts to show the results.
5.4 Constraints
•	The solution depends on the information available in the dataset.
•	The project estimates transaction value and does not predict future stock demand.
•	The model cannot make predictions using information that is not available in the dataset.
5.5 Risks
•	Poor data quality can affect the results.
•	Missing values can reduce the amount of usable data.
•	The model may not perform well if the data does not represent future transactions.
•	Using variables that directly calculate the target can cause data leakage.
 5.6 Sales Regression (Multiple Linear Regression)
      Machine Learning Type
•	Supervised machine learning
•	Regression (predicts a continuous number)
•	Algorithm: Multiple Linear Regression
       One-Hot Encoding
•	Done with pd.get_dummies (X, columns=categorical_features, drop_first=True, dtype=int)
•	Each text category becomes its own column of 0 or 1
•	drop_first=True avoids the dummy variable trap
•	Applied to: sales_channel, payment_method, region
•	The prediction tool uses the same encoding, so new data matches the trained model
       Method of Converting Words to Numbers
•	One-hot encoding for text categories
•	Date feature extraction: transaction_date converted with pd.to_datetime(errors="coerce"), then split into year, month, day, day_of_week
•	Prediction tool: menu choices (1, 2, 3...) are first mapped to category names using if/elif statements and dictionaries
       Features Used
•	Numeric: quantity, unit_price, discount_pct, year, month, day, day_of_week
•	Categorical: sales_channel, payment_method, region
•	Target: sales_amount
•	Excluded: gross_sales and discount_amount (calculated from sales data, so they would leak the answer)
       Saving and Loading the Model
•	Saving (training script): 
o	The trained model is saved with joblib.dump(model, "sales_model.pkl")
o	The list of feature column names is saved with joblib. dump(X.columns.tolist(), "model_features.pkl")
o	Both files are saved in the same folder as the program (found with os.path)
•	Loading (prediction tool): 
o	Checks that both .pkl files exist before running
o	Loads the model with joblib.load("sales_model.pkl")
o	Loads the feature names with joblib.load("model_features.pkl")
o	Uses reindex (columns=model_features, fill_value=0) so the new data has the same columns, in the same order, as training
o	Makes a prediction with model.predict(); no new training happens
